# Shader variant contract tasks

Source proposal: [ShaderVariantContracts.proposal.md](ShaderVariantContracts.proposal.md), approved 2026-09-22. The proposal is authoritative; this plan covers its remaining implementation work.

## Goal and constraints

Implement immutable, typed program/stage contracts shared by offline compilation and runtime selection. Register every owned program and fixture, compile each distinct stage configuration once, and preserve working programs until replacements link successfully.

Keep `GpuBindingContract` responsible for resource slots and interface locations, the existing TinyAst/GLSL schema and `ShaderSyntaxTreePreprocessor` responsible for source processing, and `Rendering/Spirv` responsible for binary loading. Keep the composition root thin and declarations in domain-owned files. No new project or general configuration framework is required.

Runtime manifests, macro discovery, custom binary reflection, compiler-obedience checks, runtime source hashing, and GLSL fallback are already removed or excluded; do not restore them. Preserve read-only span loading, active-resource queries, normal GL lifecycle diagnostics, and existing incremental build receipts. Ordinary uniforms and per-frame buffer data remain outside variant settings. Do not add output-directory cleanup rules.

All build/test execution must use a validation subagent. Preserve unrelated worktree/index changes. Record source review, unit tests, build, GPU behavior, package, and any live-game evidence separately; existing receipts are a baseline, not proof of these changes.

## Assumptions and decisions to resolve

- Type and API names in the proposal are provisional; settle exact names during phase 2 without changing responsibilities.
- Numeric ranges, explicit supported subsets, and per-program variant budgets must come from the setting/source inventory in phase 1. Do not guess new restrictions that reject currently supported behavior.
- Use the proposed scalar set where supported by the source pipeline; record any unsupported type rather than coercing it to another type.
- The plan requires automated rendering and lifecycle evidence. Live-game observations, if collected, are additional evidence; no new mandatory manual gate or performance target is inferred.
- The older `OpenGL.SPIR-V.BuildAndRuntime.todo` contains historical fallback behavior and is not authoritative for this work.

## Phase 1: Inventory programs, stages, and setting consumers

Outcome: the model and migration have a concrete inventory of supported behavior and ownership.

Inventory and task-to-proposal traceability: [ShaderVariantContracts.inventory.md](ShaderVariantContracts.inventory.md).

- [x] Inventory owned graphics/compute entry points, program owners, fixture programs, shared vertex stages, entry points, fixed defines, and binding contracts. Include isolated build-validator fixtures; exclude include-only files from the entry-point list.
- [x] Inventory runtime setting writers (`VgeShaderDefines`, `SetDefine`, global graphics configuration, compute callers) against actual shader uses and the current stage/specialization declarations.
- [x] Classify each accepted setting by type, default, aliases, domain/range, owning programs, stage use, and conditional availability. Identify obsolete/no-effect settings and their callers explicitly; do not silently register them as functional settings or silently drop live behavior.
- [x] Record supported program assignments, default assignments, proposed variant budgets, and current default/nondefault paths. Identify source reuse requiring distinct stage identities due to incompatible fixed defines or layouts.
- [x] Record the migration baseline and representative rendering/specialization scenarios, including AO alias behavior, near-field/world-probe toggles, importance-sampling overrides, shared fullscreen stages, and direct-file compute loading.

Gate:

- [x] Every owned entry point and setting writer has an explicit destination or an explained no-effect classification. Domain/budget uncertainties are recorded before enforcement; current behavior and the intentional alias-conflict change are distinguishable.

Completion evidence (2026-09-22): source inventory and second review completed; independent audit-stage-completion review confirmed all five tasks and the gate. The audit independently matched 98 source stages, 60 ordinary program entries and 243 expected binaries, and checked writer destinations, defaults/conditions, alternate pairings, fixed configuration and linked-proposal traceability. Documentation-only changes; no new build, unit, GPU, package or live-game execution was required or claimed.

## Phase 2: Typed models and pure variant resolution

Depends on phase 1. Outcome: deterministic contract construction and selection are testable without assets or a GL context.

- [ ] Introduce/refine `GpuShaderContract`, `ShaderStageContract`, `ShaderOption<T>`, `ShaderSettings`, and `ShaderVariantResolver`, each with its own responsibility. Keep bindings separate, publish immutable contracts, and isolate per-instance settings from shared defaults.
- [ ] Model explicit stage identity/source/type/entry point/bindings, program stage combinations, accepted option groups, and structural/specialization/fixed uses. Support one option being structural in one stage and specialized in another when types/domains permit it.
- [ ] Centralize typed normalization, GLSL spelling, canonical formatting, and specialization argument encoding. Cover declared booleans, finite integer/enum domains, and supported 32-bit scalar specializations; reject nonfinite floats and implicit truncation.
- [ ] Resolve aliases from declarations: equivalent alias/canonical values agree, conflicting values fail, omitted values use defaults, and compatibility `null` removes an override. Eliminate dependence on normalization through `double`.
- [ ] Implement declarative structural conditions using equality, `All`, `Any`, and `Not`; reject unknown/nonstructural references. Separate constant availability from supported configuration membership.
- [ ] Resolve Cartesian products or explicit complete assignment lists. Validate duplicates, missing/unknown values, missing defaults, invalid IDs/types/ranges, reserved/fixed-name conflicts, and variant budgets before GPU allocation or compiler invocation.
- [ ] Project one normalized program snapshot onto stages. Produce canonical stage keys, deterministic paths, and typed specialization arguments; exclude aliases, unrelated settings, and numeric values from binary identity. Retain inactive user selections while omitting their effective stage inputs.
- [ ] Validate shared stage definitions and output-path uniqueness. Deduplicate by stage identity and effective structural configuration without consumer-order dependence.
- [ ] Add focused unit tests for normalization/encoding, alias conflicts, independent settings instances, defaults/domains, conditional constants, explicit subsets, unknown identities/options, path determinism/collisions, fragment-only changes, stage sharing, and budget limits.

Gate:

- [ ] Pure tests demonstrate typed, deterministic resolution and useful program/stage/option/value errors. No asset reads, GL calls, binary inspection, or serialized load plans are introduced. Existing production callers remain operational until their migrations below.

## Phase 3: Explicit registry and domain declarations

Depends on phase 2. Outcome: a single registry describes every production and fixture program and its supported stage combinations.

- [ ] Make `GpuShaderContracts` a thin registry backed by domain-owned declarations and explicitly reused option groups. Register empty-configuration programs explicitly; reject unknown program/stage identities.
- [ ] Migrate the phase 1 inventory, including stage pairings currently embedded in `GpuProgram`, `SpirvInventoryTests.VertexFor`, and related helpers. Register shared stages once and give incompatible definitions distinct identities/paths.
- [ ] Declare canonical settings, supported domains, budgets, stage uses, stable specialization IDs, structural conditions, fixed defines, and aliases. Include the bent-normal alias on the short-range AO option.
- [ ] Declare how global graphics settings are projected through program group memberships. Distinguish known settings irrelevant to one stage/configuration from unknown explicitly supplied program settings.
- [ ] Reconcile no-effect legacy writers with their owners and remove obsolete writes where justified by the inventory. Keep shader-local helper macros/include guards outside the configuration registry.
- [ ] If intermediate callers require an adapter, derive it from the registry rather than maintaining a second authoritative catalog. Record its consumers and remove it in phase 5 once migration is verified; retain the public string setter only as the approved compatibility API.
- [ ] Add registry coverage tests for all owned entry points/fixtures, valid stage combinations, shared stage agreement, default assignments, budgets, and path uniqueness. Verify current supported selections against the recorded baseline.

Gate:

- [ ] Registry coverage is complete, including isolated validator fixtures; no unknown stage silently receives an empty contract. Defaults and supported choices preserve the inventory, with intentional compatibility changes documented.

## Phase 4: Contract-driven build and AST emission

Depends on phase 3. Outcome: the builder compiles deduplicated stage configurations from the registry and publishes unchanged compiler binaries.

- [ ] Migrate `ShaderVariantBuild` to enumerate supported program configurations, project them to stages, and compile each unique stage configuration once. Report program-combination and stage-binary counts separately. Source enumeration reports unregistered owned entry points without inventing contracts.
- [ ] Update `ShaderVariantSource` to use typed fixed/structural values and active specialization declarations. Use shared condition evaluation and stable IDs, preserving macro mappings and omitting inactive declarations/overrides.
- [ ] Retain import expansion through `ShaderSyntaxTreePreprocessor`, then use existing VGE GLSL schema/TinyAst editing to insert configuration before uses and guarded defaults. Keep GLSL conditional evaluation in the compiler and keep `ShaderSourceLayout` responsible for declared layouts.
- [ ] Replace the remaining derived-global-constant regex rewriting with a separate syntax-aware implementation. Inspect existing schema specializations before extending them; preserve comments, nested expressions, function-local constants, and actual source behavior.
- [ ] Preserve default and deterministic nondefault paths, strict binary publication, and build-receipt invalidation for contracts/source/fixed defines/bindings/tool inputs. Keep the receipt out of runtime assets; do not add manifests or binary instruction parsing.
- [ ] Add source-emission tests for typed macros, conditional specializations, guarded imported defaults, and derived-global-constant transformations. Assert our transformations rather than interpreting compiler binaries.
- [ ] Update/run `ValidateBuildContract.ps1` with registered fixtures and the new inventory. Verify unchanged compiler bytes, incremental invalidation, deduplication, complete asset coverage, and fresh package contents without runtime metadata.
- [ ] Run the normal shader-enabled build and compile all declared configurations. Attribute any change from the baseline inventory to deliberate declarations/deduplication rather than silently losing variants.

Gate:

- [ ] Every declared stage configuration compiles once, every expected binary exists at its resolved path, and the source/build/package checks pass. Runtime can consume the new output through the verified transition path; no temporary parallel catalogs are required.

## Phase 5: Runtime settings, loading, and reload migration

Depends on phase 4. Outcome: graphics and compute preparation consume one typed settings snapshot and preserve installed programs on failure.

- [ ] Route `GpuProgram` and compute entry points through explicit program contracts and the shared resolver. Migrate asset and direct-file fixture callers without inferring stage identities from filenames or requiring adjacent metadata.
- [ ] Expose typed setting updates and retain `SetDefine` as a contract-backed name/alias adapter. Validate updates before GPU preparation; apply global settings through declared memberships and preserve inactive selections.
- [ ] Resolve all stages from one immutable settings snapshot into a transient load plan: binary selections plus typed specialization arguments. Keep asset I/O in the span-based loader and GL object ownership in existing shader/program abstractions.
- [ ] Coalesce updates through existing scheduling. Compare effective binary/specialization selections so equivalent aliases, unused values, and unchanged settings do not reload; structural/active numeric changes prepare a replacement.
- [ ] Keep requested settings distinct from installed settings. Commit the new program and installed snapshot only after successful linking; release failed candidates and retain the working program/interface on read, specialization, or link failure.
- [ ] Preserve active-resource lookup and normal lifecycle diagnostics without compiler-obedience validation. Keep ordinary uniform/buffer setters outside the variant API.
- [ ] Remove transitional registry adapters, duplicated stage-selection switches, string specialization types, callback conditions, hard-coded alias handling, and obsolete setting discovery remnants after consumer verification. Keep the approved compatibility setter.
- [ ] Add/run settings and GPU lifecycle tests for no-op changes, alias/null handling, fragment-only choices, active numeric changes, disabling/re-enabling preserved values, coherent multistage snapshots, failed replacement ownership, repeated disposal/reload, span slices, and direct-file compute loading.

Gate:

- [ ] Both graphics and compute paths use the same resolver/build declarations. Effective no-op updates avoid preparation, successful reloads install coherent settings, and failed reloads preserve the prior program without leaked candidate resources.

## Phase 6: Registry-driven coverage and developer guidance

Depends on phase 5. Outcome: the complete supported system is verified through the declared registry and documented for future shader additions.

- [ ] Derive stage inventory and program-link cases from the registry; remove filename pairing heuristics and manifest-driven assumptions. Ensure every declared finite stage variant and supported graphics combination is exercised.
- [ ] Run representative numeric specialization defaults/boundaries and rendering regressions selected in phase 1, including world-probe/near-field visibility, importance sampling, uniform/buffer behavior, and graphics/compute reload/disposal. Do not claim exhaustive coverage of continuous/unbounded numeric domains.
- [ ] Run final normal build, unit/source/integration tests, GPU coverage, isolated build-contract verification, and a fresh package check. Record expected versus actual configuration counts, failures/skips and their disposition, and receipts separately by evidence type.
- [ ] Confirm packaging includes every resolved binary, unchanged compiler output, and no runtime manifests or build receipts. Confirm no runtime GLSL fallback, binary parser, source-hash check, or new per-frame validation was introduced.
- [ ] Update developer guidance for adding a program/stage, sharing a stage, declaring typed options/domains/aliases/conditions, changing settings, and diagnosing selection or load failures. Document the intentional alias-conflict behavior and any inventoried obsolete setting removals.
- [ ] Update proposal implementation status and this plan's evidence, clearly distinguishing completed behavior from any unresolved gap. Record live-game observations only if actually performed; do not turn test timings into performance claims.

Gate:

- [ ] All registered configurations have build/link evidence; representative rendering and lifecycle regressions pass, package coverage is verified, and no undocumented transitional path remains.

## Final acceptance

- [ ] Every owned program/stage and application-configurable option has an explicit typed declaration and stage use; unknown settings/identities fail clearly.
- [ ] Build/runtime resolution agrees, stage sharing is deterministic, and supported configurations preserve documented defaults and behavior.
- [ ] The approved no-manifest/no-custom-reflection architecture, shared AST pipeline, span loading, and safe replacement ownership are preserved.
- [ ] Required source, unit, build, GPU, package, migration cleanup, and developer-documentation evidence is recorded; unresolved gaps remain unchecked.

